igure>
<p>We know that if we were to use a data structure such as temp, <strong>it=
=E2=80=99d have to be a stack</strong>, since the problem specifies only st=
acks can be used. In this temporary data structure, we remove values in the=
 opposite order in which we added them. In other words, it follows the LIFO=
 principle, which is conveniently how a stack works. This means we can use =
a stack for our temporary storage.</p>
<hr>
<p>Now, even though we have a solution that works, having to pop off every =
single value from the top of the stack whenever we want to access the botto=
m value is quite time-consuming. To find a way around this, let=E2=80=99s h=
ave a closer look at the state of our two stacks right after we=E2=80=99ve =
moved the stack values to temp:</p>
